														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4064219">
									

<p>I do like the Fifthist scips that take the &quot;anomalous Scientology/SeaOrg&quot; approach. The description of the ship, the idea of souls bound in service beyond their human lifetimes, the hints of either an origin on a parallel earth, or of lives erased from the memory of our world are all wonderfully spooky.</p>
<p>I've gotta agree, though, about the interview, and the log of vocalizations, dragging somewhat. The interviewee seemed too willing to just gleefully exposit. I didn't really read an emotion of, like, desperation to talk to someone, anyone, who could help him out of his situation in the way he spoke to the interviewer, or any kind of suspicion towards people who he thinks had kidnapped him in his past, either of which could have been very natural in this situation. I sort of liked the alternation between moments of clarity and the ability to give straight answers with sections of more incoherent, figurative language, like his consciousness was drifting in and out of connection with this world, but I think this could be more effective with some trimming of the more repetitive parts.</p>

							</div>

								
					
